 ### Added
 
-- `SessionStore.listFor(userId, options?)`, an optional store member that answers the summaries of a user's conversations — `sessionId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` and `entryCount` — most recent activity first, so a consumer can build a conversation list instead of only rejoining one. Nothing in the engine calls it yet: the store answers the list, and the route or frame that carries it to a client is still yours to write. `InMemorySessionStore` and `MongoSessionStore` both implement it; a store of your own may leave it out. A summary never carries a conversation's entries or its credentials. Page with `limit` (50 by default, 200 at most, and `0` answers nothing) and `before`, which takes the last summary you saw: the cursor carries both `updatedAt` and `sessionId`, so two conversations saved in the same millisecond cannot straddle a page boundary and go unlisted. See `docs/session-stores.md` § Listing a user's conversations.
+- A conversation list over the WebSocket, so a client can offer a history sidebar without a REST route of its own. A connected client sends `{type: 'list_sessions', limit?, before?}` and is answered one `{type: 'session_list', sessions}`, whose rows carry `sessionId`, `entryCount` and the two times as ISO strings, newest activity first. The list is always the authenticated connection's own: a `userId` on the frame is ignored, and the store is asked with the user the upgrade authenticated. The frame is answered off the per-session lock, so a history request does not queue behind a streaming answer, and nothing is ever pushed — a `session_list` is sent only when a client asks. Page with the same `limit` and `before` cursor `SessionStore.listFor` takes. A store that implements no `listFor` answers an empty `session_list`; one that fails answers a single `error` frame with code `SERVER_ERROR` and the socket stays open.
+- `SessionStore.listFor(userId, options?)`, an optional store member that answers the summaries of a user's conversations — `sessionId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` and `entryCount` — most recent activity first, so a consumer can build a conversation list instead of only rejoining one. The engine now carries that list to clients itself, over the WebSocket frame below. `InMemorySessionStore` and `MongoSessionStore` both implement it; a store of your own may leave it out. A summary never carries a conversation's entries or its credentials. Page with `limit` (50 by default, 200 at most, and `0` answers nothing) and `before`, which takes the last summary you saw: the cursor carries both `updatedAt` and `sessionId`, so two conversations saved in the same millisecond cannot straddle a page boundary and go unlisted. See `docs/session-stores.md` § Listing a user's conversations.
 
